WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:05.370
Season 1 of House of the Dragon is over and
now it’s time to rank the episodes.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:10.139
As usual, this is my opinion and without further
ado, let’s get into it.

00:00:10.139 --> 00:00:14.740
Number 10 - Episode 6, The Princess and the
Queen.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.789
So even though this episode is at the bottom
of the list, that doesn’t mean it’s flat-out

00:00:18.789 --> 00:00:19.789
bad.

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:23.470
It’s just, this episode had the unfortunate
circumstance of being right after a 10-year

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:24.590
time jump.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:28.790
So every development is either a re-introduction
or throwing us into something entirely new

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:31.890
that doesn’t really make sense in the given
context.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.809
The biggest rift to me is Daemon’s endpoint
in episode 5 and the starting point for this

00:00:35.809 --> 00:00:37.030
episode.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:41.250
We end with a man killing his wife, still
being a nuisance in Kingslanding, and saying

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.899
that he’s going to claim his inheritance
in getting Runestone.

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:49.039
For the latter, we never hear of this development
again and we never know if Daemon actually

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:50.420
inherited Runestone.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.539
We actively set up a conflict between Gerald
Royce challenging Daemon in court, but nothing

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:55.920
comes of this.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.949
Then Laena is now suddenly betrothed to Daemon
and they have two kids.

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:04.199
This is jarring to say the least given that
he just killed his last wife and is still

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:06.150
kind of obsessed with Rhaenyra.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.229
So any scenes here are immediately off-putting
from how much of a disconnection we have from

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:13.100
where we last left off and this point in the
story.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:17.409
The same thing applies to Rhaenyra’s relationship
with Ser Harwin and other minor plotlines

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:20.270
like Allicent’s bond with Larys and Ser
Criston.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.140
The writers are essentially skipping over
the mid-point of development for these plotlines

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.220
to the point that it dampens the impact of
their payoff.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.240
The deaths of Ser Harwin and Laena would have
been significantly stronger if we came to

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.360
know who they were.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.900
Like Laena’s death in particular was well
executed, but how can we feel that bad for

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.229
her when she was only in like 3 scenes.

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:44.130
On a plot basis, that’s very flawed, but
there are still some good scenes here that

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:48.100
makes this episode better than most of the
later seasons of Game of Thrones.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:52.790
Scenes like Rhaenyra’s long oner of childbirth
which brilliantly showing off Emma D’arcy’s

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.220
ability to act.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:58.380
Getting to see the dragon pit and the introduction
of the new kiddies were well executed.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:03.380
And the brief drama we get tied to Rhaenyra’s
kids being bastards and that complex dynamic

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:08.259
infecting the red keep was great but we needed
more time of that so it could breathe.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:12.270
So this episode if anything would have been
significantly better if there was one before

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.080
this that handled more set-up.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.170
Number 9 - Episode 5, We Light the Way.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.269
This episode is tough because it’s pretty
solid up until the ending.

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:28.080
The ending just leaves such a sour taste in
my mouth from how sloppily executed it was

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.330
and the amount of questions that are left
standing.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.890
I don’t think I necessarily need to go into
detail since I’ve talked about this scene

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:39.400
extensively on two occasions, however, I do
think I overreacted a bit in terms of Criston’s

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.290
arc as a character.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:44.519
That aspect I’ve come to like more in him
trying to redeem his honor by proposing to

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:47.170
Rhaenyra and his overall quick mental decline.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:51.540
I just wish we experienced Criston’s breakdown
through his own perspective and was more of

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.340
a rich character moment.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.880
Instead of it being a convoluted mess of confusion
shown primarily from the perspective of the

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:58.980
dinner table.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.390
Then this is also exasperated by there being
a time jump after this so any confusion you

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:06.930
have isn’t answered and we have to make
assumptions.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:11.250
That definitely sucks and this flaw essentially
stems into the next episode in which there

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.209
were a lot of plotlines that needed more scenes.

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:19.450
Now since there is a big flaw, does this episode
particularly outweigh it with any great scenes?

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:22.400
To me, that question is kind of a no.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.650
Every scene on average is pretty good within
the show, but not much else within this episode

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.200
really stands out to me.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.250
Criston’s arc is the main emphasis here,
but there are two small scenes that I think

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.670
redeem it a bit that makes it better than
episode 6.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.799
Which are Otto’s farewell to Allicent and
Viserys talking to Lyonel Strong.

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:44.689
Otto’s farewell is so juicily dramatic as
he shatters Allicent’s naivety and makes

00:03:44.689 --> 00:03:47.129
her see the truth in the danger that is Rhaneyra.

00:03:47.129 --> 00:03:51.380
It’s the first time we don’t see Otto
hold back as he brutally manipulates and scares

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:52.909
the shit out of his own daughter.

00:03:52.909 --> 00:03:57.360
And the acting from both of them is stellar
along with it being in the rain that matches

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.049
the intensity of their dialogue.

00:03:59.049 --> 00:04:02.180
And as usual Rhys Ifan’s delivery is golden.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:07.090
“If Rhaenyra succeeds him war will follow,
do you understand?”

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:11.739
Then for Visery’s monologue, him recollecting
about his life and asking Lyonel if he was

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:13.950
a good king was very sweet.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:17.130
Kind of brings up an interesting internal
struggle if it would have been better to be

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.510
remembered more for going to war, or having
a peaceful rule that people may not remember

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.190
as much.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:26.680
Essentially solidifying Viserys as someone
who cares more about the realm than his own

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:27.690
ego.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:32.000
An overall good episode that would have been
higher on my list if it wasn’t for the misfire

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.500
of the ending.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.830
Number 8 - Episode 9, The Green Council.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:43.080
I was honestly pretty torn between placing
this episode as number 9 or 8, however, I

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.391
think this episode just barely beats out We
Light the Way since the ending isn’t as

00:04:47.391 --> 00:04:50.390
bad and the opening of the episode is outstanding.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:54.870
That small council meeting has such a thick
layer of dread over it as we eagerly anticipate

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.130
what these leeches are going to do.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:02.729
Then for everything to unfold and slowly start
to deteriorate and explode was so good.

00:05:02.729 --> 00:05:06.699
Developments like Otto planning all this before
this meeting, Allicent freaking out about

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:11.400
that and their intentions, Lord Beesbury’s
death, and Westerling finally challenging

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.210
Criston made this scene consistently riveting.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.940
It’s just too bad that the scene wasn’t
longer because I could have watched what happened

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.229
there for a very long time.

00:05:20.229 --> 00:05:24.569
Because after this point I think the episode
loses some of its steam during the long search

00:05:24.569 --> 00:05:25.669
for Aegon.

00:05:25.669 --> 00:05:29.680
And that just comes down to the plot because
this plot point isn’t as interesting in

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.670
comparison to most story developments since
it’s essentially our characters wandering

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:35.250
around Flea Bottom.

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.590
That and were primarily following Erryk and
Arryk and it was very hard to track who was

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:41.550
who during their growing divide as brothers.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:46.850
Like I still don’t know which Erryk is the
good one since they have the same damn name.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:50.080
I think this whole portion of the episode
would have been better if we spent more time

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.440
in the red keep because I was infinitely more
intrigued by Otto being a menacing ruler and

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.750
forcing lords to change allegiances.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:02.040
I think most people would agree that they’d
rather see that than listen to Mysaria drone

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:07.040
on about Flea Bottom or whatever in her annoying
accent that you need to turn on subtitles

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:12.880
for.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.530
But I guess it’s nice we're getting some
world-building.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:19.500
Then going into the ending, I think Aegon
had a simple and effective arc that allowed

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.180
us to quickly sympathize with him.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:26.590
A man going from despising his existence,
not being loved, and feeling like he has no

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:32.000
place in this world to finally being accepted,
being loved, and gaining a sense of meaning

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.770
from becoming the king.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:35.729
Surprisingly a good arc.

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:38.630
Then the unfortunate Rhaneys conundrum happens.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:43.580
Again, I don’t think this is as bad as the
ending to episode 5 which I assume some people

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:47.710
will disagree on, but at least it isn’t
flat-out confusing.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:51.289
Because her reasoning for not killing the
Greens at least makes sense.

00:06:51.289 --> 00:06:55.430
It just feels like a half-thought-out moment
to try and give Rhaneys a badass event that

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:59.170
doesn’t even come across that way since
she kills hundreds of innocents.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:02.800
So it definitely needed some re-writing, in
comparison to Criston’s scene that on top

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:08.099
of needing re-writes needed additional scenes
to clear up what the hell happened.

00:07:08.099 --> 00:07:12.210
Number 7 - Episode 4, King of the Narrow Sea.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:16.410
We are essentially out of the territory of
episodes that have bad aspects that weigh

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:17.560
them down.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:21.500
For this episode, the reason why I’d put
it a bit lower in comparison to the others

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.250
is mostly the plot.

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:28.040
Rhaenyra’s lust-driven adventure with Daemon
and him dumping her wasn’t my favorite episodic

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:29.040
debacle.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.120
Firstly, it felt a bit confusing as to Daemon’s
motivations because his intention was to soil

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.810
Rhaenyra for marriage so he could marry her
himself, thus becoming king, but that plan

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:42.350
is pretty ridiculous given that Viserys would
never buy that.

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:45.680
So then was he actually lying in that moment
to Viserys?

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:49.770
Did Daemon improvise that plan to hide the
fact that he almost fell in love with Rhaenyra,

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:50.970
but stopped himself?

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:55.590
Although in episode 7, he says that he spared
Rhaenyra in that moment, so that would back

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:59.150
up his plan of ruining her image to marry
her himself.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:04.060
So yeah, that felt a little convoluted to
say the least but I still really enjoy the

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:08.750
dynamic between Rhaenyra and Daemon and their
overall night out in Kingslanding was cool.

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:13.090
That and the writers paralleled this fun adventure
with Allicent’s relationship with Viserys

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:15.720
which was devoid of life and enjoyment.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:20.410
This then leads to a romantic sex scene with
Ser Criston Cole, which at first I loved,

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:23.570
but now that I hate Ser Criston it makes me
upset seeing this.

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:26.889
That doesn’t hurt the quality of the episode,
it just makes me salty.

00:08:26.889 --> 00:08:31.430
Ironically out of all this spicy conflict,
the thing that intrigued me the most was Otto

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:34.099
having to tell Viserys the hard truth about
Rhaneyra.

00:08:34.099 --> 00:08:39.430
That made for a crazily awkward scene and
seeing Otto struggle to deliver the news was

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:42.719
so pleasing because we’ve never seen him
this worried before.

00:08:42.719 --> 00:08:46.610
Then Rhaenyra convincing Viserys to remove
Otto as hand, made for a great ending to the

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:47.610
episode.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.459
Those were my favorite aspects and the rest
was alright.

00:08:50.459 --> 00:08:54.660
So in comparison to the other episodes that
have great plot points, I’m going to like

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.899
those a bit more.

00:08:56.899 --> 00:09:01.899
Number 6 - Episode 1, The Heirs of the Dragon.

00:09:01.899 --> 00:09:06.279
Normally the first episode for different shows
tends to skew towards one of the lesser episodes

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:09.740
since they are tasked with having to do a
lot of set-up and exposition.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:12.279
But The Heirs of the Dragon doesn’t do this.

00:09:12.279 --> 00:09:16.990
Only in the opening scene do we get exposition,
and to be fair, it’s kind of necessary to

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:20.160
establish where we are in the history of Westeros.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.339
After this though, the writers essentially
plant us right into an already existing world

00:09:24.339 --> 00:09:26.110
and do very little exposition.

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:30.399
They seamlessly blend us into this narrative
and allow the audience to learn who the characters

00:09:30.399 --> 00:09:33.240
are by seeing them react to different forms
of conflict.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:38.050
No expository dialogue is needed when each
character is being molded by different obstacles

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:39.370
that test them.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:43.990
Like right away Viserys is tested by the birth
complications of his new child and then having

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:47.360
to deal with succession issues after the death
of his wife.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:51.110
Daemon trying to find some form of purpose
and dealing with the reality of not being

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:52.670
the heir anymore.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:56.709
Rhaneyra navigating through all those obstacles
while taking on the responsibility of becoming

00:09:56.709 --> 00:09:57.839
the new heir.

00:09:57.839 --> 00:10:02.350
And different side characters briefly cutting
in in each scene to try and sway the main

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:04.900
characters’ minds with their own wants and
wishes.

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:09.541
The show quickly establishes and fully develops
each of its main characters as we are thrown

00:10:09.541 --> 00:10:12.960
into an engaging story with outstanding conflict.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:17.100
Like the birth scene was so horrific and tragic
that it really sets the tone for the story

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:18.100
to come.

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:21.810
Not to mention that a lot of the dialogue
scenes were all very interesting.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:26.030
Especially when the topic of succession comes
up and we see Viserys break down and everyone

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:28.610
trying to influence him for their own benefit.

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:32.740
Then there’s also a fun tournament with
jousting to add some spectacle into the episode,

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:37.420
so overall this opening episode is well-balanced
and acts as a perfect introduction to House

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:39.440
of the Dragon.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:43.630
Number 5 - Episode 2, The Rogue Prince.

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:47.790
This may be an unpopular opinion, but I like
The Rogue Prince a little more than the first

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:48.790
episode.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:52.790
This episode opens immediately with a haunting
feeling as we first see what the Crabfeeder

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:53.860
is doing.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:58.110
This both gives us some great imagery, on
top of it giving what Corlys has to say some

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:02.680
weight because before this we didn’t really
know what was happening in the stepstones.

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:07.480
So quickly and efficiently this opening makes
us excited for this upcoming conflict.

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:12.070
This then leads into a small council meeting
and in this scene we see Rhaenyra finally

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:17.210
speak out, which essentially teases her independent
attitude and that leader quality is starting

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:18.460
to break through.

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:23.079
Not only does she have these triumphant character
moments, but we also break Rhaenyra down in

00:11:23.079 --> 00:11:27.339
two separate scenes where she deals with the
loss of her mother and Viserys having to move

00:11:27.339 --> 00:11:29.170
on and find a new wife.

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:34.220
Each of these scenes hits a beautiful emotional
depth that absorbs the audience with Milly’s

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:35.820
entrancing performance.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:38.730
This is to me, what sold the actress so well.

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:42.350
These small character moments where they simply
talk to one another and go through a vast

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:46.290
array of emotions drips with so much intrigue.

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:50.269
These scenes are also imbued with a great
point of conflict in which Viserys has to

00:11:50.269 --> 00:11:52.020
deal with having to find a new wife.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:56.899
This dynamic has a lot of depth because every
character has a stake in it and can add something

00:11:56.899 --> 00:11:57.899
new.

00:11:57.899 --> 00:12:01.540
Like everyone having their own mischievous
motivations and seeing Viserys go along with

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:04.720
some of them like talking with Laena was funny.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:08.600
Then this isn’t even mentioning the best
part, which is the tense showdown at Dragonstone

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.660
between Otto and Daemon.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:13.730
The amount of tension within this scene was
so palpable.

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:19.930
From the music slowly building up, the fog
obstructing the sun and creating a tense atmosphere,

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:25.170
how unpredictable Daemon is, and Caraxes showing
up made this scene so fun to watch.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:29.560
Then it takes an unpredictable turn where
Rhaneyra shows up to solve the confrontation

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:33.100
without any bloodshed, thus acting as her
big moment.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:37.529
And this moment also unlocks her theme that
she essentially earns here, and Rhaneyra’s

00:12:37.529 --> 00:12:40.600
theme is probably my favorite throughout the
entire show.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:44.440
The Rogue Prince is pretty great through and
through and even the cinematography is at

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:46.870
its best in my opinion.

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:51.470
Number 4 - Episode 3, Second of his Name.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:55.649
Initially, I wasn’t a huge fan of the first
half of the episode, but I’ve come to really

00:12:55.649 --> 00:12:58.699
like the journeys that both Rhaenyra and Viserys
go on.

00:12:58.699 --> 00:13:03.010
The parallels to how they both treat their
anguishes were interesting since Rhaenyra

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:08.899
chooses to run away and be rebellious while
Viserys chooses to drown himself in wine.

00:13:08.899 --> 00:13:13.030
Seeing him grapple with the annoyance of politicking
and wondering if he made the right decision

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:17.610
to make Rhaenyra his heir allows us to see
Viserys without any barriers.

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:22.230
Kings are always really reserved, but here,
he’s pouring out so many emotions that we

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:24.440
are allowed to witness and feast on.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:28.930
Like the bonfire in particular was the peak
of the episode in my opinion because of this.

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:32.620
Then Rhaneyra also boils over to a degree
and lets out all of her anger while killing

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:33.620
the boar.

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:37.839
She’s so overcome with the responsibilities
of growing up, having to marry, and being

00:13:37.839 --> 00:13:41.590
a pawn for her father in terms of politics
that it’s driving her crazy.

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:45.890
So her showing back up to the camp drenched
in blood makes her seem like a badass and

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:46.899
commands respect.

00:13:46.899 --> 00:13:51.240
I also like the small detail of Ser Harwin
being turned on by that.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:53.130
Kind of foreshadowing the future.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:57.459
This dual journey has some great character
development and even the resolution afterward

00:13:57.459 --> 00:13:59.639
in Kingslanding was pretty touching.

00:13:59.639 --> 00:14:04.410
Viserys deciding to let Rhaneyra choose who
to marry was a great compromise, but yet again,

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:07.899
that’s not necessarily a good choice to
make as a king.

00:14:07.899 --> 00:14:10.279
Then we have the stepstones conflict.

00:14:10.279 --> 00:14:14.350
To me, the best aspect of this battle is easily
Daemon Targaryen.

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:18.680
This was a great showcase for the man where
he risks everything to prove himself and even

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:20.750
turns down help from his brother.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:24.290
The rush he makes was some intense action
and I hope people have moved away from the

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.040
arrow controversy.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:29.089
Because relax, the man still gets hit like
3 times.

00:14:29.089 --> 00:14:32.940
My only issue with this portion of the episode
is the fact that it doesn’t really last

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:34.149
that long.

00:14:34.149 --> 00:14:38.880
The actual battle only lasts like 2 minutes
and then it quickly ends once Daemon walks

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.019
out with the Crabfeeder’s corpse.

00:14:41.019 --> 00:14:45.790
It gives off a bit of an anticlimactic feeling
and if they had more impressive/longer takes

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:51.120
of action following our heroes then this episode
would maybe crack my top 3 for this season.

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:56.389
But it’s still pretty good and acts as a
fun final sequence to an already great episode.

00:14:56.389 --> 00:15:00.710
Number 3 - Episode 10, The Black Queen.

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:04.660
Some people may feel compelled to put this
episode at number 1, because of the insane

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:09.529
final scene with Vagar, but I think this finale
has a bit too much set-up.

00:15:09.529 --> 00:15:11.220
And that’s in regards to wartime preparations.

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:15.949
It’s nice to see, but it’s not something
that you’d expect within a finale that’s

00:15:15.949 --> 00:15:18.160
normally packed with a lot of payoff.

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:21.149
Which the payoff we do get is amazing.

00:15:21.149 --> 00:15:25.060
Rhaenyra having a miscarriage was extremely
uncomfortable and drastically increased the

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:30.310
tension at the beginning of the episode, Rhaenyra
becoming queen was immensely uplifted by Ramin

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:35.170
Djawadi’s wonderful score, Corlys finally
allying himself with Rhaneyra was so satisfying

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:40.290
when his theme played, and the death of Lucerys
was batshit insanity and had some of the best

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:43.020
action and tension build-up we’ve ever seen.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:47.240
If the episode was just these scenes with
maybe 1 set-up scene cut out or even just

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:51.449
the ending 10-minute sequence on repeat, then
It would be hard to not put this episode at

00:15:51.449 --> 00:15:52.519
number 1.

00:15:52.519 --> 00:15:57.310
But since there is a lot of round-table discussions
within the episode, it slows down the fast

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:58.740
and fun pace.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:02.410
Cutting out one of the set-up scenes could
be better to keep the pace moving more quickly,

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:06.230
but it’s hard to say that since they are
needed and are still good.

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:10.079
This is obviously a minor issue and these
are the things I need to address when the

00:16:10.079 --> 00:16:12.690
top 3 episodes are so close in quality.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:16.860
I can honestly argue why this episode and
the next two could be number 1 and at the

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.339
end of the day it will come down to opinion.

00:16:19.339 --> 00:16:23.100
One thing that we can all agree on though
is how perfect the ending of the season is.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:28.110
Aemond chasing Lucerys on Vagar was so intense
given the size difference and the setting

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:29.480
of the scene.

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:34.470
Hiding away this massive threat in the thundering
clouds was terrifying and the action was perfectly

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:38.490
presented by not hiding the VFX in terrible
lighting conditions.

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:42.740
The fact that we can follow the action with
ease is refreshing and it never breaks our

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:48.620
immersion. 10 minutes of pure adrenaline and
this scene alone carries the episode.

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:52.720
Number 2 - Episode 7, Driftmark.

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:57.130
When this episode was released it was my favorite
and it almost retained that spot.

00:16:57.130 --> 00:17:01.149
The reason it was there in the first place
is that this episode has a perfect collection

00:17:01.149 --> 00:17:02.470
of scenes.

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:06.789
Nothing feels out of place, there’s no downbeat,
and it just keeps getting better.

00:17:06.789 --> 00:17:10.360
The episode opens strong with an ensemble
of characters interacting with each other.

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:14.500
I tend to love these scenes because of how
much depth is here and the number of small

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:17.780
interactions that happens between every character.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:21.589
Everyone has a moment to shine here and the
writers efficiently set up certain dynamics

00:17:21.589 --> 00:17:23.630
that will be integral later on.

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:27.060
Then we have an engrossing debate between
Rhaenys and Corlys.

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:32.049
Their motivations perfectly contrast with
each other which allows for some great dialogue.

00:17:32.049 --> 00:17:34.260
Two lines in this scene are even quoted in
the trailer.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:37.320
And then, we have Rhanerya and Daemon on the
beach.

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:42.300
Now I like the writing of this scene, it’s
just that, due to the dark lighting, we can’t

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:44.880
really see their reactions that well.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:48.820
Kind of feels like a hindrance in an otherwise
integral scene between these two characters

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:53.000
who are getting emotional, but that’s the
only small flaw within this episode.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:57.870
This leads into Aemond taming Vagar and this
was a great display of awe-inspiring scale.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:01.809
The action as Aemond tries to desperately
hold on was so gripping.

00:18:01.809 --> 00:18:05.540
This is followed by Aemond having a violent
confrontation with Rhaenyra’s children,

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:09.039
which I honestly did not know how that scene
was going to play out.

00:18:09.039 --> 00:18:12.980
So when Aemond loses an eye I was genuinely
surprised.

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:17.250
This sets up the best scene of the episode
in which Allicent demands justice for that.

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:20.180
So many aspects about this scene are amazing.

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:25.000
We have Viserys desperately trying to keep
everyone at bay, the awkward silence as everyone

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:29.900
acknowledges the truth about Rhaenrya’s
children, the harsh back-and-forth dialogue,

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:34.429
and the explosive ending as Allicent rushes
Rhaenyra with Viserys’ dagger.

00:18:34.429 --> 00:18:39.520
Truly a great climax and somehow the episode
doesn’t end here and ends with a crazy encore

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:41.490
of Laenor’s fake-out death.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:45.440
Which even that scene has some impressive
writing in that the writers lead you to believe

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:49.570
that Laenor actually dies, but doesn’t and
gets a happy ending.

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:55.570
This episode is flat-out stacked and has great
character work, action, plot twists, love,

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:58.980
and even Otto Hightower gets reinstated as
hand.

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:02.160
Easily top 2 worthy and is really close to
being in my top spot.

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:06.299
But the next episode has something very special
about it.

00:19:06.299 --> 00:19:10.799
Number 1 - Episode 8, The Lord of the Tides.

00:19:10.799 --> 00:19:13.880
Yeah, you know why this episode is at number
1.

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:18.350
The last supper is easily the best scene in
the season and is a pure rush of happiness

00:19:18.350 --> 00:19:19.820
every time I watch it.

00:19:19.820 --> 00:19:23.240
I don’t know how many people here watch
Rick and Morty, but I’m like the Rick who’s

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:28.059
held prisoner as they make him watch the same
happy scene over and over to farm Serotonin,

00:19:28.059 --> 00:19:31.370
but for me, that scene on replay is Viserys
smiling.

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:35.200
It’s such a purely wholesome moment that
I couldn’t help but shed a tear when first

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:36.550
watching it.

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:40.360
Throughout the entire season, we come to love
Viserys and the tragedy of his entire bodily

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:43.190
decay makes us feel so bad for him.

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:47.429
So seeing this last moment in which he gets
to witness his family be happy one more time

00:19:47.429 --> 00:19:52.470
is extremely precious and is not something
I was expecting to come out of this dark show.

00:19:52.470 --> 00:19:56.659
That and the rest of this scene is still great
in which he reveals his face to everyone and

00:19:56.659 --> 00:19:58.809
gives a heartwarming speech.

00:19:58.809 --> 00:20:01.970
Along with Rhaenyra trying to reconcile with
Allicent.

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:05.220
This is the perfect example of the calm before
the storm.

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:08.570
Then for the rest of the episode, there’s
really no down moment.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:12.620
Rhaenyra and Daemon visit Viserys while the
man is in agony, Allicent having to deal with

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:17.330
a handmaid getting raped by Aegon which in
particular was a great showcase for the handmaid

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:22.880
actor in terms of acting, Allicent roasting
Aegon and him being douche, Rhaenyra awkwardly

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:27.700
meeting up with Rhaenys after killing her
son, Viserys’ makeup routine, and some other

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:30.309
quick scenes that are all equally intriguing.

00:20:30.309 --> 00:20:34.340
However, this wouldn’t be in my number one
spot without another standout scene, which

00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:37.780
is the throne room debate for the succession
of Driftmark.

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:41.580
Throughout this entire scene, you know this
hearing is rigged in Vaemond’s favor so

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:46.440
you have no idea how Rhaenyra will come out
of this situation on top and I assumed she

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:47.440
wouldn’t.

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:51.669
So to have Vizzy T show up like an absolute
legend works as an excellent twist and it’s

00:20:51.669 --> 00:20:55.350
also severely uplifted by Ramin Djawadi going
ham on the score.

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:59.940
And I also love the fact that the director
draws this moment out so much by having Viserys

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:02.440
slowly walk across the entire throne room.

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:06.050
Really emphasizing how much of an impressive
feat this is.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:09.169
Then Vaemond explodes and Daemon removes his
head.

00:21:09.169 --> 00:21:13.880
So overall great escalation in terms of plot
points and twists within this scene and it

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.480
just keeps getting better as you go through
it.

00:21:16.480 --> 00:21:20.380
With this shocking death, triumphant moment
for Viserys, one of the most heartwarming

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:24.410
moments in Game of Thrones history, and a
collection of solid scenes in between those

00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:28.630
bangers makes this a GOATED episode of House
of the dragon.

00:21:28.630 --> 00:21:32.090
Thank you for watching and let me know in
the comments what your ranking would be.

